<article class="env-lemma" id="08YE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08YE">Lemma <span data-tag="08YE">5.17.8</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ be a continuous map of topological spaces. If $f$ is bijective, $X$ is quasi-compact, and $Y$ is Hausdorff, then $f$ is a homeomorphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It suffices to prove $f$ is closed, because this implies that $f^{-1}$ is continuous. If $T \subset X$ is closed, then $T$ is quasi-compact by Lemma <a href="/tag/005C" data-tag="005C">5.12.3</a>, hence $f(T)$ is quasi-compact by Lemma <a href="/tag/04Z9" data-tag="04Z9">5.12.7</a>, hence $f(T)$ is closed by Lemma <a href="/tag/08YB" data-tag="08YB">5.12.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>